[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  Mara's eyebrows lift as you place two gold coins on the scarred table between 
  you. They catch the copper light, gleaming against the dark wood. She looks 
  at them. Then at you. A beat of silence. Then she laughs — a short, genuine 
  sound, surprising in the quiet tavern. "Two gold." She picks them up, turns 
  one between her fingers. "You know, most people try to pay me *after* the 
  job's done. Or not at all." She pockets the coins with a small shake of her 
  head. "You're either very trusting, very desperate, or very smart." She taps 
  the table once. "Alright, Kael. You've bought my time and my word. Let's earn 
  that gold." She gestures to the charcoal rubbing — the spiral with seven 
  breaks. "Here's where I've been stuck. Seven nights. Seven passes through the 
  gate. But the ashfall stops for exactly seventeen minutes at midnight — so 
  whoever's doing this has a seventeen-minute window, and they're using every 
  second of it." She leans in. "Old Bram is dead. His keys are gone. So how are 
  they opening the gate?" She lets the question hang. "I've got one lead I 
  haven't followed yet. The night watch at the north wall — one of them 
  might've seen something. He drinks at the *Drowned Bell* down by the wharf, 
  third shift. Name's Rourke." She tilts her head. "Or we go to the gate 
  itself. Look at it in daylight. See if there's something I missed in the 
  dark." **Your call. Rourke, or the gate?**... 
