[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  The obsidian key is heavier than it looks. As your fingers close around it, 
  the cold bites deep — deeper than stone has any right to. For a moment, it's 
  like holding a shard of winter. You slip it into your pack. The weight 
  settles against the brass compass, two strange bedfellows in the leather 
  dark. The copper lamp *steadies*. The shadows slide back to their proper 
  places. Mara exhales — she'd been holding her breath. "Good." She rolls her 
  shoulders, breaking the tension. "That's one piece of the puzzle accounted 
  for." She stands, buttoning her charcoal coat. "I've been sitting on my heels 
  for three nights, Kael. Watching ash fall and shadows move. I'm done 
  watching." She looks at you, sharp-eyed and ready. "Rourke will be finishing 
  his third shift at dawn. If we head to the Drowned Bell now, we can catch him 
  before he drinks himself to sleep. Or — " She glances toward the shuttered 
  window. Beyond it, the faint grey of morning presses against the glass. "— we 
  can walk to the gate. See what daylight shows us that the dark has been 
  hiding." She waits, hand resting on the back of her chair. **Which way?**... 
