[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  You turn your face toward the smoke-stained rafters and let the name fall 
  from your lips. "Old Bram." The name hangs in the air. The copper lamp 
  *creaks* on its chain. Ash scratches at the glass. No answer. You didn't 
  expect one. But the silence itself is an answer. The empty space where his 
  voice should be — gruff, warning, half-drunk on cheap ale — that absence 
  presses against your chest like a cold hand. You close your eyes. And you 
  remember. *The ferryman's words, rough as gravel, spat across a scarred table 
  much like this one:* *"The Ash Gate don't just keep things out, boy. It keeps 
  things in. And when it opens — and it will open — you won't hear a key 
  turning. You'll hear something breathing on the other side that ain't been 
  breathing for a long, long time."* *He'd leaned forward then, the candlelight 
  carving deep shadows into his face.* *"Don't go looking for what's behind it. 
  It's already looking for you."* The memory fades. The tavern is quiet. The 
  front door *shudders* under another round of knocks. The grey man behind the 
  bar clears his throat. His voice is dry as old bone. "You gonna answer that, 
  or do I tell 'em the place is empty?" 

