[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  You're about to step closer to the handprint when a voice cuts through the 
  stillness behind you. "You took your time." You turn. She's standing where 
  the alley meets the gate's clearing, half-swallowed by shadow. Young — early 
  twenties maybe. Lean frame wrapped in a patched leather coat that's seen 
  better years. Dark hair cropped short and uneven, like she cut it herself 
  with a knife. A satchel slung across her chest, one strap frayed. She's 
  holding a folded piece of paper between two fingers. She watches you with 
  pale eyes that don't blink. You introduce yourself. Her head tilts a 
  fraction. "Sable Quill." She says it flat, like it's a formality she's tired 
  of repeating. "And I wasn't waiting for you specifically. I was waiting for 
  whoever came through here first." She tosses the paper. It flips once in the 
  still air and lands at your feet. "Message came for you three days ago. Old 
  Bram paid me a silver to deliver it before he died." She nods toward the 
  folded paper. "I don't read 'em. I just carry 'em." Her eyes drift past you 
  to the Ash Gate. Something flickers in them — recognition? Wariness? "So. You 
  gonna read it, or do I stand here all morning?" 

